where there was no business,

00:03:19.366 --> 00:03:21.717
of course they were effected by that.

00:03:21.717 --> 00:03:23.318
But your hustle, your work ethic,

00:03:23.318 --> 00:03:27.932
your drive is not
predicated on your zip code.

00:03:28.332 --> 00:03:30.969
- [Voiceover] Tony asks,
"Gary, what are your thoughts

00:03:30.969 --> 00:03:33.198
about people that say
companies like Twitter, Uber,

00:03:33.198 --> 00:03:36.153
and Snapchat are way
overvalued in the market?"

00:03:38.039 --> 00:03:40.014
- Tony, what's really
interesting about this quesiton

00:03:40.014 --> 00:03:41.627
is I am one of those people.

00:03:41.627 --> 00:03:44.750
I grew up in the late and mid 2000s

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.047
into the 2010s 11s, 12s, 13s

00:03:47.047 --> 00:03:48.663
thinking a lot of things were overvalued.

00:03:48.663 --> 00:03:49.764
I thought we were at a little bit

00:03:49.764 --> 00:03:50.949
of a bubble in 2010, 11.

00:03:50.949 --> 00:03:53.574
I've been proven to be extremely wrong.

00:03:53.574 --> 00:03:56.244
What I would tell you is,
is that inflation happens.

00:03:56.244 --> 00:03:58.274
The world changes.

00:03:58.274 --> 00:03:59.993
I'm starting to wrap my head the notion

00:03:59.993 --> 00:04:02.419
of wait a minute maybe
these things are undervalued

00:04:02.419 --> 00:04:05.171
because the value of a
hundred million dollar compnay

00:04:05.171 --> 00:04:08.213
just 15, 20, 30 years
ago was a far grander

00:04:08.213 --> 00:04:10.930
and wealth just contines
to exponentially grow

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:15.534
and so I would say that,
it's a double-edged sword.

00:04:15.534 --> 00:04:17.765
There wil be prenty of
things over valued, tons.

00:04:17.765 --> 00:04:20.029
But if something is proven to have won

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:22.618
and you think is the
future of infrastrucure.

00:04:22.618 --> 00:04:25.576
A la an Uber, where you
can clearly see them getting

00:04:25.576 --> 00:04:28.179
into FedEx and UPS lanes in other places.

00:04:28.179 --> 00:04:31.156
Plenty of people have lost a lot of money

00:04:31.156 --> 00:04:33.176
by thinking things were overpriced.

00:04:33.176 --> 00:04:35.753
And I want you to listen to
that backwards one more time.

00:04:35.753 --> 00:04:38.749
So rewind it whether you're
listening or watching.

00:04:38.749 --> 00:04:41.457
A lot of people have lost
a lot of money by thinking

00:04:41.457 --> 00:04:42.606
things have been overvalued.

00:04:42.606 --> 00:04:43.991
And of course there's
the ying to that yang

00:04:43.991 --> 00:04:45.801
and pople have lost in
betting on overvalued things

00:04:45.801 --> 00:04:49.043
but when things hit scale Amazon and Ebay

00:04:49.043 --> 00:04:50.855
in the early days of the internet

00:04:50.855 --> 00:04:52.294
where people thought they were overvalued

00:04:52.294 --> 00:04:54.361
but the world caught up to them.

00:04:54.361 --> 00:04:57.240
Be careful if the world is
catching up to something.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.562
What I mean by that is
if you start watching

00:04:59.562 --> 00:05:02.023
normal people doing things
on some of these platforms

00:05:02.023 --> 00:05:03.753
you're going to see a scale jump

00:05:03.753 --> 00:05:06.006
Are they overvalued?

00:05:06.006 --> 00:05:08.142
If there's a crash in the market

00:05:08.142 --> 00:05:11.311
or a terrorist attack in a
major city, can it be overvalued?

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:15.491
Of course, but is that
temporary is the real question.

00:05:15.491 --> 00:05:17.871
And when I say temporary
don't misunderstand me.

00:05:17.871 --> 00:05:20.472
Is it overvalued and
will go down for a year

00:05:20.472 --> 00:05:22.373
but then go back up because they're living

00:05:22.373 --> 00:05:24.880
in the future while other things are not.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.260
- [Voiceover] Annie Hubbard asks

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.687
"For blogs just starting
out, how do I get people

00:05:29.687 --> 00:05:32.979
to be interviewed without
being a big name media company?"

00:05:35.675 --> 00:05:37.210
- Annie your question really means

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:40.008
to me, which is how do I go out

00:05:40.008 --> 00:05:42.226
and get people where I can
take their brand equity

00:05:42.226 --> 00:05:43.874
as a starting point for mine, right?

00:05:43.874 --> 00:05:45.268
Like if I could interview Gary Vee

00:05:45.268 --> 00:05:47.485
and put it out there some
of his fans will come over,

00:05:47.485 --> 00:05:48.681
listen to that interview and

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:49.900
then they'll become aware of me.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:53.476
So what you're talking about
is equity leveraging arbitrage.

00:05:53.476 --> 00:05:58.716
Brilliant, agree with it, for
the people that are listening.

00:05:58.723 --> 00:06:01.684
If you look at podcast land
that's how they all do it.

00:06:01.684 --> 00:06:03.319
I did a bunch of those interviews

00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:04.346
promoting Jab, Jab, Jab, Right Hook

00:06:04.346 --> 00:06:05.646
and then they're leveraging my name over

00:06:05.646 --> 00:06:08.072
and over and over again
to build auidience.

00:06:08.072 --> 00:06:09.790
So it is a classic game.

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.927
There's a dirty little secret Annie.

00:06:11.927 --> 00:06:15.619
People that have leverage and have fame

00:06:15.619 --> 00:06:18.206
even if you call it z-level fame, like me.

00:06:19.376 --> 00:06:22.197
They like to talk and so I'm going

00:06:22.197 --> 00:06:23.522
to tell you something that kind of refers

00:06:23.522 --> 00:06:25.612
to question number one on this episode

00:06:25.612 --> 00:06:27.911
which is the answer is hustle.

00:06:27.911 --> 00:06:31.487
I would just keep asking
and asking and asking.

00:06:31.487 --> 00:06:34.285
I would audit all the
places that look like you

00:06:34.285 --> 00:06:36.572
or kind of like you and look at the people

00:06:36.572 --> 00:06:37.965
that have done interviews there

00:06:37.965 --> 00:06:40.310
and when you start realizing that I

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:43.062
and Tim Ferriss and Seth Godin like

00:06:43.062 --> 00:06:45.185
to give interviews because
we also like the exposure.

00:06:45.185 --> 00:06:47.775
We also have been successful
and we like to give back.

00:06:47.775 --> 00:06:50.200
I loved, a couple of
years ago, or last year,

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.338
my interview 365 day year thing

00:06:52.338 --> 00:06:53.777
where I just gave people interviews

00:06:53.777 --> 00:06:55.020
that didn't even have any audience

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:56.297
because I was giving back.

00:06:56.297 --> 00:06:59.002
Some don't want to give
interviews unless you're big.

00:06:59.002 --> 00:07:01.754
That's why you get nos others do it

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.911
because they like to pay
back to the community

00:07:03.911 --> 00:07:06.444
and they remember grinding and the person

00:07:06.444 --> 00:07:07.663
that gave them a break.

00:07:07.663 --> 00:07:10.217
And so very honestly I
would ask and oh by the way,

00:07:10.217 --> 00:07:11.901
I got a real curveball for you

00:07:11.901 --> 00:07:13.991
and this is for everybody listening.

00:07:13.991 --> 00:07:16.322
Sometimes you need to ask
the same person 11 times.

00:07:16.322 --> 00:07:17.892
And one could say that
you're being annoying

00:07:17.892 --> 00:07:19.655
but if you come from a good place

00:07:19.655 --> 00:07:21.453
and you do it with a prideful tack

00:07:21.453 --> 00:07:22.895
and you do it in an appropriate way

00:07:22.895 --> 00:07:24.463
and if you're actually listening

00:07:24.463 --> 00:07:25.868
to what's going on in their world

00:07:25.868 --> 00:07:27.586
maybe Tim's got a new book coming out.

00:07:27.586 --> 00:07:29.444
Maybe he redesigned his website.

00:07:29.444 --> 00:07:30.866
Maybe he's got a new podcast.

00:07:30.866 --> 00:07:32.938
Those are times to strike like a cobra

00:07:32.938 --> 00:07:34.167
when they want something

00:07:34.167 --> 00:07:37.379
and you want something,
then interests align.

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:40.345
- [Voiceover] NCG asks "What does one do

00:07:40.345 --> 00:07:42.319
when you feel you have unique content

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:44.246
but not tapping into the right audience

00:07:44.246 --> 00:07:46.052
or not gaining visibility."

00:07:48.819 --> 00:07:51.292
- Nikola, this is where you start
looking yourself in the mirror

00:07:51.292 --> 00:07:54.753
and deciding if you have
business development chops.

00:07:54.753 --> 00:07:57.679
This notion that you
have the greatest content

00:07:57.679 --> 00:08:01.404
and it's not finding
it's audience is romantic

00:08:01.404 --> 00:08:04.773
at worst and audacious at best.

00:08:05.883 --> 00:08:09.016
To me what you need to
look at is are you capable

00:08:09.016 --> 00:08:11.011
of also building audience for your content

00:08:11.011 --> 00:08:12.951
or are you just the content provider

00:08:12.951 --> 00:08:15.041
and do you need a partner
who can help you go out

00:08:15.041 --> 00:08:18.610
and do that or have
you just lost all sense

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:21.763
of reality and you're
stuff is just average.

00:08:22.662 --> 00:08:25.170
- [Voiceover] Galen asks, "At
what point do you just get rid

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:28.415
of a client because they are
just an all around clown?"

00:08:29.651 --> 00:08:31.172
- Galen, this is a funny question.

00:08:31.172 --> 00:08:33.587
I think a lot of VaynerMedia 
employees are going

00:08:33.587 --> 00:08:34.818
to have a fun time with this quesiton

00:08:34.818 --> 00:08:36.374
if they are watching or listening.

00:08:36.374 --> 00:08:41.265
I think that for me I
hold on for dear life.

00:08:41.275 --> 00:08:43.679
First of all business is business.

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:46.223
I think one of the reasons
people recommend getting rid

00:08:46.223 --> 00:08:49.553
of clients is they don't have
the stomach for adversity.

00:08:49.553 --> 00:08:51.482
I actually can deal with the negativity

00:08:51.482 --> 00:08:52.713
and it's business is business.

00:08:52.713 --> 00:08:55.186
If they fire me then I
deserved to be fired.

00:08:55.186 --> 00:08:57.307
But the notion of there
are clients to be fired.

00:08:57.307 --> 00:08:59.295
It's right and a lot of
times it helps your business

00:08:59.295 --> 00:09:01.047
and so it's about self-awareness.

00:09:01.047 --> 00:09:04.834
So for me, Galen, it's
very late in the process,

00:09:04.834 --> 00:09:06.807
like almost borderline, I don't know,

00:09:06.807 --> 00:09:09.025
when they take a knife and
stab it through your eye.

00:09:09.025 --> 00:09:11.080
It might not be a good
relationship anymore

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.238
but that's literally the level
that I'm looking forward to

00:09:14.238 --> 00:09:17.465
because I can handle it and
it's not slowing me down.

00:09:17.465 --> 00:09:19.114
We've got plenty of clients that I think

00:09:19.114 --> 00:09:21.353
by all modern standards
should have been fired

00:09:21.353 --> 00:09:23.827
but I have not and it has
not hindered the growth

00:09:23.827 --> 00:09:27.008
because yes there's some time
and attention put into that

00:09:27.008 --> 00:09:29.224
and even in a world where
we get lots of new business.

00:09:29.224 --> 00:09:32.082
There is value in retention.
There's also moods.

00:09:32.842 --> 00:09:35.494
How about relationships?
Do you just get divorced

00:09:35.494 --> 00:09:36.898
when a time comes tough?

00:09:36.898 --> 00:09:39.615
The ebb and flow. Do you
just get rid of a best friend

00:09:39.615 --> 00:09:40.961
because of a bad night?

00:09:40.961 --> 00:09:45.258
Do you get rid of friend
after six straight bad nights?

00:09:45.258 --> 00:09:48.497
One could argue yes but I'd
say hmm there's a lot there

00:09:48.497 --> 00:09:49.936
that you may want to fight for it.

00:09:49.936 --> 00:09:51.492
So I think it comes
down to self-awareness.

00:09:51.492 --> 00:09:54.290
A lot of people are not capable

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:57.657
of dealing with the conflict
or it brings them down.

00:09:57.657 --> 00:10:00.118
To me, I like to rise to that challenge.

00:10:00.118 --> 00:10:02.522
I like that game but it comes down

00:10:02.522 --> 00:10:04.739
to my own self-awareness of me as a leader

00:10:04.739 --> 00:10:07.108
and more importantly i have
to factor in the empathy

00:10:07.108 --> 00:10:10.312
of 400 other people and
is it bringing them down.

00:10:10.312 --> 00:10:12.738
So cool, I can stomach it but
as they're getting yelled at

00:10:12.738 --> 00:10:14.631
on the phone every single day,

00:10:14.631 --> 00:10:16.605
is it bringing them to a
place where they want to leave

00:10:16.605 --> 00:10:19.019
and so for me it's when
it's effecting other people

00:10:19.019 --> 00:10:20.808
that are in my family that I value more

00:10:20.808 --> 00:10:22.454
and family I mean my company

00:10:22.454 --> 00:10:24.465
that I value more than the client itself.

00:10:24.465 --> 00:10:26.578
That's when I'm looking at
it but then a lot of times,

00:10:26.578 --> 00:10:28.342
I'm trying to surround,
interchange and account people

00:10:28.342 --> 00:10:30.026
of the people that have more of my stomach

00:10:30.026 --> 00:10:33.718
and can handle it.
To me, that is the timing.

